  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🌑 Dark Elf Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Drow names with a house and a title, for the elves who went below: Matron Mother Quenira of House Mal'Morr."
    },
    "intro": "Dark elf names are sharp and sibilant, full of <em>z</em>, <em>x</em> and <em>ph</em>, and they rarely stand alone: a drow is their house before they are anything else. This **dark elf name generator** gives you drow first names, male and female, with a noble house and a title to match, from Weapons Master to Matron Mother.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who the dark elves are",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>In D&amp;D</strong>, dark elves are the drow, who live in the Underdark and worship the spider goddess Lolth. Their society is ruled by women: the Matron Mothers of the great houses and the priestesses beneath them.</li><li><strong>In Tolkien</strong>, \"Dark Elves\" meant something gentler: <a href=\"elf-name-generator.html\">elves</a> who never saw the light of Valinor. Not evil, just elves who stayed behind.</li><li><strong>In the Elder Scrolls</strong>, the Dunmer are dark elves too, and not underground at all. See the <a href=\"dunmer-name-generator.html\">Dunmer Name Generator</a>.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Houses and titles",
        "content": "<p>Drow cities like Menzoberranzan are ruled by noble houses, ranked against each other and always plotting. A drow is introduced by house, and the house name often comes with an apostrophe, as in Drizzt Do'Urden.</p><ul><li><strong>Female titles</strong>: Matron Mother (the head of the house), High Priestess, First Daughter.</li><li><strong>Male titles</strong>: Weapons Master (in charge of the house's fighters), Archmage, Patron (the Matron Mother's consort).</li></ul><p>None of the house names here belong to a house from the Forgotten Realms, so you can rank yours anywhere you like.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Playing a drow",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Decide where you stand with your house.</strong> Loyal, exiled, or secretly working against it: every drow story starts there.</li><li><strong>Female drow outrank male drow</strong> in traditional drow society. A male drow with ambition, or a female drow who walked away from power, is an instant character.</li><li><strong>The surface is strange.</strong> Sunlight blinds them in many settings. A drow who has come up into the light has given something up to do it.</li></ul>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "Are dark elves the same as drow?",
        "answer": "In D&D, yes: drow is the dark elves' own name. In other settings, dark elves can be quite different, like the Dunmer of the Elder Scrolls."
      },
      {
        "question": "Are these names from the Forgotten Realms books?",
        "answer": "No. They're in the drow style, but famous drow like Drizzt, Jarlaxle and Viconia, and the great houses like Baenre and Do'Urden, are left out."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I use these names in D&D?",
        "answer": "Yes, they're free to use and you don't need to credit the site."
      }
    ]
  }
}
